,0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8413\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8461\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8643\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8665\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8849\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8888\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23386dfb-4266-50f7-b8bd-add2ea98080d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499451 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.5,\\frac{0.08}{40}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.5,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =P(\\frac{0.51-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{0.55-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.22<Z<1.12) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.12)-P(Z<0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.22)=0.5871, \\\\ Φ(1.12)=0.8686.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22) \\\\  & =0.8686-0.5871 \\\\  & =0.2815.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499462 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499462 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499462 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499462 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{148}$ be a random sample of size $n=148$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =98$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =74.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2843\\,$ | $\\,0.2810\\,$ | $\\,0.2776\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ | $\\,0.3156\\,$ | $\\,0.3121\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ | $\\,0.3520\\,$ | $\\,0.3483\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "404778b6-2da1-56e5-aa20-574c3d5c8b29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499462 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=148,\\,\\mu =98,\\,\\sigma =74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(98,\\frac{{74}^{2}}{148}) \\\\  & ∼N(98,37).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =P(Z>\\frac{95-98}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.49) & =1-P(Z<-0.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.49)=0.3121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =1-Φ(-0.49) \\\\  & =1-0.3121 \\\\  & =0.6879.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499476 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{90}$ be a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =92$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =30.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $90$ and $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ae46753-9928-51e1-ad50-7c5a5efcdfe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499476 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=90,\\,\\mu =92,\\,\\sigma =30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(92,\\frac{{30}^{2}}{90}) \\\\  & ∼N(92,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =P(\\frac{90-92}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{95-92}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.63<Z<0.95) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.95)-P(Z<-0.63) \\\\  & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(0.63) & =0.7357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63) & =1-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =1-0.7357 \\\\  & =0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =0.8289-0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499534 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =250$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =64.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $260.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ | $\\,0.9699\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5ea7895-e704-5589-b13a-69dbf76447fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499534 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=128,\\,\\mu =250,\\,\\sigma =64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(250,\\frac{{64}^{2}}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(250,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<260).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =P(Z<\\frac{260-250}{\\sqrt{32}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.77) \\\\  & =Φ(1.77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.77)=0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =Φ(1.77) \\\\  & =0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499537 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-3.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | 